As a result of these very elementary considerations, the injective spaces could be called *absolute retracts*, if one remembers to modify the standard definitions by using *arbitrary* subspaces rather than just *closed* subspaces. Note too that it is easy to show that the only continuous maps $e: X \to Y$ for which the extension property

<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="320" height="210" viewBox="0 0 320 210" role="img" aria-label="Extension triangle: X to Y by e, f from X to O, dashed f bar from Y to O.">
  <g font-family="Georgia, 'Times New Roman', Times, serif" fill="currentColor">
  <text x="72" y="50" font-size="22" font-style="italic" text-anchor="middle">X</text>
  <text x="248" y="50" font-size="22" font-style="italic" text-anchor="middle">Y</text>
  <text x="160" y="192" font-size="22" text-anchor="middle">&#x1D546;</text>
  <text x="160" y="28" font-size="18" font-style="italic" text-anchor="middle">e</text>
  <text x="8" y="118" font-size="22" font-style="italic">f</text>
  <text x="292" y="118" font-size="22" font-style="italic">f</text>
  <line x1="300" y1="105" x2="318" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25"/>
  <line x1="86" y1="52" x2="234" y2="52" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/>
  <polygon points="246,52 233,47.6 233,56.4" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/>
  <line x1="72" y1="62" x2="144" y2="162" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/>
  <polygon points="152,172 145.8,159.2 139.2,165.8" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/>
